 (report . "APPROVED. Round 8 (independent, read-only VERIFY subagent) certified the repaired v1.00.30 W4 wave at implementation head c6254a66ca6967d43ad81524ed3ea784f0775fec with content digest 8902bbb6b7adfd5aeb94c71e7280c80dd0bb53aa717606c163115e6283be841c, with no blocking findings. The round-7 contradiction is resolved in the retained evidence: artifacts/ci-recovery/v1.00.30-w4/raw/verify-chain.txt:230-231 carries only the two benign canonical-form notes for this artifact dir and no SHA256SUMS drift note; the capture is green (focused 184 tests, arch/security/workflows/deps/metrics/provenance exits 0, fast 1208/1208); sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS passes for all six bound files. The report labels ba35b090f as the Repaired code / drill head and distinguishes the schema-2 implementation head, both ancestors carrying the recorded content, and the drill transcript, the structured eager-fallback-ms block and the report prose all agree on 472/481/480 ms. The reviewer confirmed the full acceptance surface: the bounded eager gate with the restore record and upload ordered after it, the two-axis telemetry classification with unobserved costs omitted, the launcher usable-root telemetry uploaded after the shard, fast-pilot-only activation with no broadening, auto handled in both CLIs with every other value failing closed, the trusted-root excluded from checkout materialization while its path stays exposed, the W2 checkpoint pin cascade, and the required-check schema-2 identity. No regression against main's v1.00.31 W1 invocation and per-module build/run contract was found. Rounds 1-7 findings (producer input, zero-module run, linear history, W2 hygiene, lane wiring, off-lane eager gate and honest W4 telemetry, stale artifact heads, record-before-gate ordering, drill-timing single-sourcing, and the SHA256SUMS-consistent capture) are all fixed and recorded in the red-first captures and the repair narrative. The wave is approved for merge through the protected gate with the operator merge authorization bound to this exact head. The implementation PR's CI then surfaced two latent post-squash defects in already-published state, both repaired at c6254a66ca6967d43ad81524ed3ea784f0775fec: the W6 rehearsal matrix recorded a pre-squash W6 branch tip as its rehearsal head, which became unfetchable when the branch was deleted at wave close (re-stamped to the published W6 binding commit fc1a6fa36 with byte-identical rehearsal inputs, generator rule documented, workflows job on fetch-depth 0), and the round-6 eager-gate timing rewrite tripped the W1 invocation contract's not-a-script guard (gate restored to a single-line raco make main.rkt with the cost bracketed by stamp steps). Round 9 (focused delta review) then found that the round-8 fix had also edited the matrix generator, which is itself a recorded rehearsal input, so its recorded input digest no longer matched the committed bytes; the generator was restored byte-identical to its W6 form (the recorded-head operational rule lives in the rehearsal report, which is SHA256SUMS-bound and not an inputs-digest entry), SHA256SUMS was rebound over the changed report, and every recorded rehearsal-input digest is truthful again. The final delta (generator restore, rebound sums, refreshed chain capture) is committed at c6254a66ca6967d43ad81524ed3ea784f0775fec and a final focused delta review is solicited against it; this entry carries that verdict (and its narrative) before the implementation pull request is merged. VERDICT: APPROVED. The final focused delta review (independent, read-only VERIFY subagent) found no blocking findings in the delta dd18e1560e91ee8243dbc787eba9734f101f2849..c6254a66ca6967d43ad81524ed3ea784f0775fec, and the round-8 approval still holds for the unchanged surface. It verified with concrete repros that the W6 rehearsal head is re-stamped consistently across the matrix and the report to the published commit fc1a6fa36 (the old pre-squash tip is now only historical prose), that every recorded rehearsal-input digest still equals the current file digest, that the W6 SHA256SUMS verifies, and that the test's own recorded-head, inputs and SHA256SUMS checks remain intact and unweakened; that the eager gate is restored to an extractor-visible single-line `run: raco make main.rkt` with its cost bracketed by always-run stamp steps, the restore record still ordered after the gate, the fallback cost emitted only when the gate actually ran, and the ordering assertion retained; that the workflows job checks out with fetch-depth 0; that the retained chain capture is green (184 focused tests, arch 32/32, security 64/64, workflows 33/33, check-deps, metrics --lint, provenance lint, fast 1208/1208) with SHA256SUMS verifying and no drift note for either artifact dir; that the drill transcript, structured block and report prose still agree on 472/481/480 ms; and that the schema-2 trio identity is coherent at implementation head c6254a66ca6967d43ad81524ed3ea784f0775fec with content digest 8902bbb6b7adfd5aeb94c71e7280c80dd0bb53aa717606c163115e6283be841c. One non-blocking observation was noted (the start stamp runs in the normal step flow rather than being explicitly marked if: always(), which does not affect the repaired gate contract). The wave is approved for merge through the protected gate with the operator merge authorization bound to this exact head. The implementation pull request's own CI then exposed a third CI-only defect that local execution cannot reach: the fast-shard launch step passed --telemetry-file into $RUNNER_TEMP/compiled-root-map, but the resolution step that creates that directory is skipped whenever the compiled-root lane is off (the default), so the launcher failed closed with open-output-file on the off lane and every test shard failed even though all 403 files and 5989 tests passed. Reproduced locally (launcher exits non-zero at ci/prepared-environment/compiled-root.rkt:753 without the directory; with it the child runs and the telemetry file is written), fixed at c6254a66ca6967d43ad81524ed3ea784f0775fec by creating the map directory in the shard step before launching (so usable-root telemetry is produced on every lane and the post-shard upload captures it), marking the eager-gate start stamp if: always() for a reliable cost bracket, and adding a committed test that asserts the shard step creates the map directory before the launcher runs. A final focused CI-delta review is solicited against this head; this entry carries that verdict and its narrative before the implementation pull request is merged."))